A1. Most people lose this sometime or other at the dentist's. ~ NERVE
A5. The drinks are often on this. ~ BAR
A7. These are usually stronger than horseradish, but Bebe and Billie just love 'em. ~ PIPES
A11. Where detectives put their hats when they call. ~ ON
A12. This is not so good. ~ EVIL
A13. Prohibitionists are having a hard time trying to make this kind of a place of the U. S. A. ~ ARID
A14. Xanthie Illumination (init.). ~ XI
A15. What the well dressed girl dresses with. ~ TASTE
A17. This is always cutting up. ~ ADZ
A19. These always stick around—the insects! ~ PESTS
A21. This always has the last word. ~ ECHO
A22. This digs up the dirt. ~ SCOOP
A24. Primitive ancestors. ~ APES
A25. An eye sore. ~ STY
A26. This old bird likes dives. ~ AUK
A27. The bulls don't like this. ~ RED
A29. This is indefinite. ~ ANY
A30. A hot dog wrapper. ~ BUN
A32. That long fish again. ~ EEL
A33. A racket (not Helen Wills'). ~ ADO
A35. You're apt to get these with a roast. ~ SKEWERS
A38. What husbands do for a night off. ~ ASK
A40. Something to do for a good old soak. ~ RET
A41. Not so well. ~ AIL
A42. You could mop up with this. ~ SOP
A43. This often puts on the English. ~ CUE
A45. Hard to find. ~ ELUSIVE
A48. A heavy weight. ~ TON
A50. This comes up in Congress occasionally. ~ IRE
A51. A heavenly body. ~ ORB
A52. You have to do this constantly at night clubs. ~ DIG
A54. A newt. ~ EFT
A56. Not a mice thing to throw. ~ FIT
A57. Female joyriders should carry one of these. ~ MAP
A59. Oh! So sad. ~ ALAS
A61. Just a little warm. ~ TEPID
A63. A priestly caste of ancient Persia. ~ MAGI
A64. A state of rigidity. ~ RIGOR
A66. A listening-in device. ~ EAR
A67. This is always loaded. ~ LADEN
A68. Kinetic Zygodactyle (init.). ~ KZ
A69. One of your sister's cousin's grandfather's daughters. ~ AUNT
A70. A kingdom, south of Assyria, mentioned in Genesis. ~ ELAM
A71. Do this and you won't do wrong (abbr.). ~ RT
A72. Any young swain would do this for the girl friend. ~ YEARN
A73. This is carried on the shoulder (not a chip). ~ HOD
A74. This could be used to "tie that bull." ~ LASSO

D1. These have words but do not talk. ~ NOTES
D2. What Congress has done to altogether too many bills. ~ ENACTED
D3. This is very forbidding. ~ VETO
D4. The only only girl. ~ EVE
D5. This is very dark. ~ BLACK
D6. A little shaver. ~ RAZOR
D7. A fowl disease. ~ PIP
D8. One of these wouldn't strike a dumb man. ~ IDEA
D9. This goes farther. ~ EXTENDS
D10. An effeminate he-male. ~ SISSY
D16. Flappers have been called everything but this. ~ SHY
D18. To accomplish. ~ DO
D20. You should go here on the water-wagon. ~ SPA
D22. What the fellow did who got in over his head. ~ SUNK
D23. A most noble fellow. ~ PEER
D26. Stern (nothing to do with a boat). ~ AUSTERE
D28. Something sweet that follows the eats. ~ DESSERT
D31. A big cheese in Switzerland. ~ SWISS
D33. A part of the best circles. ~ ARC
D34. Something gold-diggers go in for. ~ ORE
D36. French water. ~ EAU
D37. Yale. ~ ELI
D38. If you've this—you're apt to get ahead. (There's a tough one.) ~ APT
D39. What Harvard men live on. ~ KIN
D44. To employ for some useful purpose. ~ UTILIZE
D46. This is not right—ever. ~ LEFT
D47. Empty. ~ VOID
D49. Wild asses. ~ ONAGERS
D52. A colorful gentleman. ~ DARKY
D53. This would make any woman wild. ~ GAG
D55. These are always biting on something. ~ TEETH
D56. What the dry Congressmen will get. ~ FIRED
D57. They say some hot dogs get this way. ~ MAD
D58. A pied animal. ~ PINTO
D60. What high flyers often do. ~ SOAR
D62. This would be your aunt's sister's husband. ~ PA
D63. And this is your aunt's sister's husband's wife. ~ MAMA
D65. It's hard to get a used car to do this. ~ RUN
D67. Little Animated Lilliputians (init.). ~ LAL


